 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n \cdot k)$ per sample, where $n$ is the number of octaves and $k$ is the cost of the base noise function (constant for fixed lattice size) [S3][S7, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(P + G)$, where $P$ is the permutation table size (256 or 512 integers) and $G$ is the gradient vector array size (256 vectors) [S5, S9, S10, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Linear with the number of octaves. Higher octaves increase detail but also computational cost [S3][S7, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Generates continuous, smooth, pseudo-random fields with multi-scale detail suitable for natural phenomena [S3][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Trilinear interpolation can cause grid artifacts (visible as straight lines) which may require box filtering or higher-order interpolation [S19, unofficial]. Hash functions using trigonometry are not portable across all hardware [S15, unofficial]. Single-octave implementations lack fractal detail [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Procedural texture generation, volumetric cloud/terrain synthesis, and any application requiring smooth, non-repetitive noise fields [S3][S5, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When strict portability of hash-based noise is required without permutation tables [S15, unofficial], or when sharp, non-smooth features are needed (use Ridged Multifractal instead) [S18, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(O \cdot N)$, where $O$ is the number of octaves and $N$ is the number of evaluation points (voxels or ray samples). Each octave requires a constant number of hash lookups and interpolations [S3][S6, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for on-the-fly evaluation (GPU shaders) or $O(D^3)$ for pre-computed 3D textures, where $D$ is the dimension of the noise map [S15, S17, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Default octaves in compute shaders are often 4 [S8, unofficial]. Typical FBM octave count is 4–7 [S12, S16, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Generates continuous, smooth, and natural-looking stochastic patterns with details at multiple scales [S3][S18, unofficial]. Efficient to evaluate on GPUs [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Single-octave variants lack detail [S1, unofficial]. Hash functions may vary across hardware [S17, unofficial]. Requires careful normalization to maintain consistent amplitude across octaves [S6, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Procedural texture generation, volumetric cloud/terrain synthesis, and implicit surface definition [S3][S16, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When discrete, cellular structures are needed (use Worley) or when sharp ridges are required (use Ridged Multifractal) [S2, S20, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Origin: Ken Perlin introduced Perlin noise in 1985 [S17, unofficial]. Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) was popularized in computer graphics by Perlin and others for generating natural textures [S3]. The method is widely used in computer graphics to create stochastic patterns with details at potentially many different scales [S3]. It is used in voxel world generation, cloud rendering, and terrain synthesis [S1, S14, S15, unofficial].
+Originating from Ken Perlin's work on gradient noise, FBM was introduced as a method to create fractal-like noise by summing multiple octaves of a base noise function [S3]. It has been used extensively in computer graphics to create stochastic patterns with details at potentially many different scales [S3]. The technique is foundational for procedural generation in games and film, enabling the creation of complex natural phenomena like clouds, terrain, and smoke from simple mathematical functions [S4, S16, unofficial].
 
